Opinion by Leslie Runnels
Thanks to City Council members Latoya Cantrell and Jason Williams, New Orleans is about to do something about its longstanding housing issues. Everyone knows that rents have risen dramatically in the past few years, which has provided landlords with more income. But many apartments are still unsafe with neglected repairs and maintenance problems.
The Healthy Homes ordinance ensures that all New Orleans rentals provide a minimum standard of health and safety -- so that we no longer have families paying a majority of their income for a home that is falling apart and creating health and safety risks for the neighborhood.
As a landlord, I appreciate the Healthy Homes initiative -- also known as the rental registry -- because as someone who takes a great deal of pride in my properties and my tenants, I face unfair competition from those who don't. While it would be nice to stop spending money on repairs on my apartments, that is not how you run a business; you have to make repairs to protect your investments to reap the rewards.
If a landlord refuses to make even the most basic repairs, tenants currently have two options: fix it themselves or move out. But that hits our working class families the hardest. When a family is paying more than half their income in rent, there's not much left over for basic necessities, let alone repairs or moving expenses. So they put up with it and live in dangerous, unhealthy conditions.
If you can't keep your tenant's children from falling through the floor or a working smoke detector in your apartments, maybe it's time to get out of the landlord business. We have more than 40,000 children living in rentals citywide, and more than a third of households are paying more than 50 percent of their income towards rent. How much do you think is left over to fix faulty wiring, holes in the roof or other basics that keep families and children safe? And how many times do we have to hear stories about tenants asking landlords for basic repairs, only to be evicted because tenant protections in our state are the worst in the nation? Providing a safe home is the responsibility of the landowner, not the tenant.
I'm invested in my neighborhood and know that everyone loses when neglect creates fire hazards and other dangerous conditions. We can't expect children or adults to be successful students, musicians, teachers or even public servants when they come home to mold-infested apartments teaming with mosquitos because the windows and doors don't seal.
During the short-term rental debate, we heard a lot about how much worse things have gotten for New Orleans renters over the past 10 years. While we've been debating this for a long time, we now have an opportunity to translate these sentiments into action and improve the health, safety and quality of life for our renters. By providing incentives for landlords to get their homes up to a basic standard over the course of the next few years, we can ensure that all New Orleanians have access to a safe, decent place to live.
Tenant advocates like the Greater New Orleans Housing Alliance and{g} the{r} New{r} Orleans{g} Ten{r}ants{g} Union{r} have{r} been{g} working{r} hard{r} to{r} get{g} this{g} ordinance{g} passed{g}.{r} We{r}'re{g} excited{r} to{g} see{g} it{g} become{r} law{g}.{g}
