The animation is digitally photorealistic and pretty gorgeous; the directors John Musker and Ron Clements worked on "The Little Mermaid" and "Aladdin," but those films (which have a lot to recommend them, however un-P.C. their messaging) belong to their recent time and place, and this one to ours.
The score's the thing here, buoyant and often delightful. Miranda collaborated with Mark Mancina and Opetaia Foa'i on the overall musical tapestry, which owes a little something to "Hamilton," a little to the Alan Menken/Howard Ashman Disney animation heyday, a little to the sounds of the islands. Johnson (who can rap, at least in small doses) manages the Broadway-ized ode to preening self-regard with{g} a{g}pl{r}omb{g}.{g}
