Helping more than 24,000 couples get married since 1988, Nancy Campana knows a thing or two about preparing for the big day.
That includes hosting what the owner of Nouveau Event Planning calls ��The Big One,�� the 29th annual Wedding Extravaganza ��16 Show set for the weekend of January 16-17, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. Held at the Caboto Club in Windsor, it is the largest wedding show in southern Ontario and one of the longest established events of its kind in Canada.
The theme of this winter��s wedding show is Light of Love. For happy couples in a hurry, the theme could be the light speed of love as they check out more than 200 businesses including caterers, bakers, decorators, florists, musicians, photographers and other wedding suppliers on hand, ready to help plan the entire celebration.
Campana expects there will be many couples on a mission. ��They will come to the show and book their wedding from start to finish,�� she says. Noting that 25 to 30 per cent of marriage proposals happen in the week between Christmas and New Year��s, engaged couples are eager to get their vision underway.
Other show attendees will be wrapping up the plans they put in motion last year, seeing what various cake bakers, invitation designers and honeymoon travel agents have to offer. Couples choosing to do most of the work themselves also appreciate being able to compare the services of the select wedding specialists they need to hire.
The average length of an engagement in Canada is 14 to 18 months. ��The older the couple are, the shorter the engagement tends to be,�� says Campana, noting the average age of wedding couples is 30 to 32.
��Get married in eight months to a year,�� the show producer advises.
That is the ideal length of time, Campana believes. ��The longer the engagement, the more money you will spend. There will be opportunity to keep changing your mind.��
Having a budget is a sound idea, considering the typical Canadian couple spends $30,717 on its 129-guest wedding and the honeymoon, according to a
WeddingBells.ca
latest survey.
That kind of budget is healthy for local halls and other businesses serving the wedding industry. The Big One gives them a platform to shine, while subtly letting couples know that planning their joyous union should be enjoyable, not overly stressful.
The first indication that wedding planning can be fun is the show��s LOVE Instagram corner, where couples may pose in front of giant, illuminated letters spelling out ��LOVE.�� They can then upload their photos to try and win a $500 Devonshire Mall gift card.
In the Interactive Wedding Gallery, ��we try to appeal to all the senses,�� Campana says. Live musicians play while attendees sample delectable treats from local caterers, bakers, coffee bars and banquet venues. Tabletop décor displays reveal the freshest looks in wedding design. Attendees can guess honeymoon destinations in the travel quiz corner.
During the fashion shows at 11:30 a.m., 1:30 and 3:30 p.m. both Saturday and Sunday, fashions will be highlighted for the entire wedding party with the 1:30 show each day featuring fashions exclusively for bridesmaids, Mother of the bride/groom and groomsmen.
Amidst emerging wedding trends, technology ranks high. ��Something we haven��t seen before now at the show is drone photography and video,�� Campana says.
A remote-controlled drone flies high and zooms in low to capture the wedding action from unique perspectives. Video taken earlier of the ceremony can be played during the reception.
