Jackie Taylor thinks nothing of taking credit for the weather.
"Look at this day I brought you," the indefatigable Taylor told her audience at Black Ensemble Theater on a recent, weirdly balmy Sunday afternoon. Everyone chuckled warmly. At Jackie Taylor's Uptown joint, the founder is expected to make the sun come out.
For some 23 years in the basement of an old Hull House, that's exactly what Taylor has done.
Sure, Black Ensemble has done a great deal to bring attention to the art and struggles of African-American musicians -- the current show, "I Gotcha," celebrates the life and work of Joe Tex, Wilson Pickett, Ben E. King and Solomon Burke. While other arts groups may shout about diversity and togetherness, there's no question that Black Ensemble is one of the very few Chicago cultural institutions where folks of different races hang out together at every performance of every show. And Taylor, who has an eye for talent, has made a number of careers, including that of the Broadway performer Chester Gregory II, who cut his teeth at Black Ensemble on "The Jackie Wilson Story."
But when they inscribe Taylor's tombstone (and given the energy level of this 57-year-old, that will likely be sometime in the middle of the 23rd Century), they should focus on her greatest achievement: The eradication of negativity and the dispensing of a very good time. Time and time again.
If you're not enjoying yourself at Black Ensemble, you are to be pitied. "We are in a period where people need this message more than ever before," Taylor says. And she reaches more than 60,000 people each season.
Which is not to say Taylor has not had her troubles. Born in Cabrini-Green, she is most certainly a self-made woman. It's probably fair to say that her theater, which has an annual budget of a little under $2 million, is for regular people, distinct from the economic and political elites who help some of Chicago's other groups prosper. And her facility has long been inadequate. In January, the whole theater flooded, ruining Taylor's set and delaying her "I Gotcha" opening.
