London��s dump is getting a $4.4 million facelift, and it will need another one in about three years.
As the landfill south of London fills with garbage, the city is preparing to approve its next phase, excavating a new 6.2 hectare portion of the landfill, called a cell. The new cell is expected to last about 3.5 years until the next, and final cell needs to be excavated, at a similar cost.
That��s $8.8 million of taxpayers money, approximately, in less than four years to handle London��s growing garbage pile.
��This work is essential. I am not sure $4.4 million is an enormous amount of money. We have to expand this site,�� said Coun. Harold Usher, in whose ward the W12A landfill is located. He also sits on the civic works committee, which will discuss the report Tuesday.
��This has been coming to us for a while. It is necessary. There is nowhere else to go.��
The excavating of the new cell will create about 1.1 million square metres of disposal capacity, states the report, but it will fill quickly.
��It is definitely a worthwhile expense. It is an investment. This is never cheap.��
This report is not about a landfill expansion, but filling what already has been approved. An expansion is being studied and will be needed in about seven years, said Michael Losee, solid waste division manger with the city.
The money for the project is from the federal gas tax reserve fund and work is expected to start in June, and be finished by early fall.
