Felix Lanciault, a six-year-old boy in Laval, Que. was crushed when his teacher disqualified him from a classroom raffle to win a teddy bear because his lunch was in a plastic sandwich bag. Confronted by the upset parents, the teacher apparently shrugged that, ��We have to take care of our planet�� — which apparently warrants such measures. But don��t we already expect our public schools to instill in our children certain social and political values and behaviours? Or is this taking things too far?
Kevin Libin in Calgary
: The main reason this story is so outrageous isn��t because of the values the school is teaching, but because of the method it��s using to teach them. Shaming and ostracizing a little boy because of a choice his parents unwittingly made (it seems the school neglected to inform them about their new rules) is just plain low. I��ve been struck before how often hard-core environmentalists are oblivious to the sensitivities of kids — from forced showings in school of the apocalyptic An Inconvenient Truth that leave kids awake with
nightmares about death
to the recent British ad campaign that
literally shows children murdered
for not signing on to the global warming crusade.
If this teacher had been more sensitive, this just wouldn��t be a story. Most people would find the lesson itself unobjectionable. We do expect our schools to inculcate our kids with dominant cultural values, even dubious ones. We expect them to caution against drug use, and against unprotected teen sex. We expect them to praise democracy and condemn dictators. Trouble is, not every parent wants her kid learning that masturbation is normal. Not every parent wants her kid raised a capitalist. And not every parent (including me) wants his child being taught that humans are a polluting cancer on the planet. Send your kids to a public school, though, and they��ll be forced to absorb prevailing public values. It��s yet another reason why school choice—letting families decide which kind of values they want their child learning in the classroom — is the freest, fairest way to ensure all parents have the right to raise their children in the best way they know how.
Barbara Kay in Montreal:
I��m glad Kevin brought up the shocking ad that unwittingly got to the fascist heart of the eco-warrior movement: the film shows the environmental apparatchiks�� hatred of those infidels, and the fantasy harboured by the anointed ones to exterminate such blots on their environmental landscape.
I find our public education system very confusing. Teachers are always bemoaning the cruelty of standardized testing of objective knowledge, because it allegedly makes the lower achievers feel bad about themselves. On the other, we have this show trial of a kid whose only ��crime�� is to bring a sandwich in a baggie. The sad part of the story is that the kid was already tuned into the Soviet-era shaming he knew he��d be in for and begged his mother for a Tupperware container. He should never have been made to feel so anxious. So the crime wasn��t even his: it was his evil parents�� gaffe that got him into trouble.
If the public schools have decided that environmentalism will be on the curriculum, let the teachers follow the guidelines as set down in their manuals. And if they want to be inventive about games or role-playing or whatever, fine. And if the rule is that litter that comes to school goes home with the kids, fine. But to single out any child for censure because of a baggie speaks to an institutional culture in which teachers see themselves as judges rather than educators. According to this teacher��s curious strain of logic, other kids could be punished for their parents�� driving them to school when they could easily have walked. In my grandchildren��s school, that would mean 90% of the kids being shamed.
Matt Gurney in Toronto:
I happen to know through my own personal connections that a lot of the teachers don��t believe in the environmental lessons they��re teaching, or don��t have any opinion on the matter at all, and just basically read what they have to to fulfill their obligations. The absurdity of shaming a child for such a non-event, especially given the energy costs and detergent wastes necessary to clean Tupperware sandwich containers, is evident. But the incident in question reflects one teacher��s failure to use good judgment more so than it does the failings of the education curriculum. As Kevin says, teachers are always going to have to teach what they��re ordered to, and obviously, that will shift with time. The only thing that can matter in the classroom is how well the teacher does. This whole event honestly seems a more logical connection to the recent debate over merit pay for teachers than it does any environmental aspects.
Kevin Libin:
Actually, both the merit pay question and the teaching of values question are resolved when you give parents choice in how their kids are schooled. Public school has a warm, fuzzy feeling to it, but it is without question one of the most effective tools a government has to promote dominant cultural values. I keep a close eye on what my kids learn about the environment at school, and I deprogram them when they��re told that humans are destroying the planet or that we need to ��save�� trees. I completely believe Matt��s report that most teachers are just reading what��s in front of them. So much of it isn��t even sensible, like this silly business about a disposable Ziploc. We��re hardly short of room for garbage: all the trash Canadians produce in the next 1,000 years will fit into a landfill just a few square miles in area and 100 feet deep (which can eventually be turned into a park). These are baseless, brainless environmental gestures. If we spent more time teaching our kids critical thinking and science, and less time on this meaningless green fluffiness, you can be sure they��d be able to figure that out for themselves.
