Letters to the editor from Highland Park, Mundelein, Libertyville, Lake Bluff and Lake Forest.
Painting in retirement
Tribune columnist Barbara Brotman recently wrote "Taking up painting not just for ex-presidents; Many come or return to the easel only later in life" (News, April 14).
A few year ago, at age 88, I enrolled in an art class given at our senior center. We used acrylic paints, and we could draw and paint whatever we wished.
It wasn't easy, and when my teacher asked, "Mary, are you having fun?" I answered, "I'm struggling."
President George W. Bush hung his paintings in his library. Mine are hanging in the bathroom.
Painting is a wonderful thing for seniors. Remember Grandma Moses!
— Mary Lewis, Highland Park
Tax loopholes
In response to people stating that they will not shop at Walgreen if the company moves its headquarters to a more tax-friendly country, I would ask how many of them have Apple products in their possession? Apple is one of the largest companies that take advantage of the United States tax loopholes. So these people should not use their Apple products either.
Walgreen Co., in this instance, at least, has been transparent with this movement.
It would be no surprise to learn of other very large U.S. corporations that use this strategy to enhance their bottom lines. Isn't it the United States government that should be the focus of our anger? After all, the taxpayer is the one who has to make up the difference in the shortfalls.
— Joyce Jeffries, Mundelein
Charter schools
The Tribune and others have been pushing for more charter schools in the Chicago area, but folks need to understand the fact about these schools. According to a study conducted by the Stanford University Center for Research on Education Outcomes, charter students, on the whole, end the year with reading skills eight instructional days ahead of public school students, and perform at about the same level as public school students in math.
We also need to understand the underlying causes of schools performing poorly: namely, poor school funding and poverty. Many students who live in dysfunctional families and/or neighborhoods filled with crime and poverty generally do not perform well in school.
