Scientists concerned over hunting of grizzly bears in British Columbia have taken their case to the journal Science, claiming the province is increasing killing opportunities without having any precise fix on the bear population.
The B.C. government has estimated a population of 13,000 to 14,000 grizzlies spread over the vast province, but researchers say the number could be as low as 8,000.
��The problem of B.C. grizzly bear management is that the uncertainty is simply ignored: Although the government maintains their targets are conservative, a simple comparison between their own limits and their own records of kill rates show that is clearly not the case,�� argued Kyle Artelle, a researcher at Simon Fraser University.
The province��s annual grizzly kill was 50 bears in 2001. The newly elected (not-very-liberal) Liberal Party government lifted a hunting moratorium. The number of recorded kills rose to 350 in the next six years.
The province last year opened to grizzly hunting new areas, particularly in the Coast Range and Kootenay region of Southeast British Columbia.
British Columbia does have protected areas, the most famous the Khutzeymateen Bear Sanctuary in a valley north of Prince Rupert. But even such places the ��Khutz�� has been under pressure from firms exploring proposed pipeline and transmission corridors.
In the Purcell Mountains, in southeast B.C., a giant proposed ski resort near Jumbo Pass threatens to put a big obstacle in north-south migration of a healthy grizzly bear population. Grizzlies are frequently seen in meadows around the pass, and in the neighboring Purcell Wilderness Conservancy.
The province claims that B.C.��s grizzlies can sustain an annual mortality rate of as high as 6 percent, counting not only hunting but bears killed on highways or ��problem bears�� shot by ranchers. The Liberals�� government virtually wiped out regional game management a decade ago, so the amount of poaching is only a guess.
Four leading scientific researchers, including Artelle, have signed a letter questioning the province��s estimates and expanded killing zones.
Aboriginal First Nations groups, along the British Columbia coast, have banned killing of grizzlies in their ancestral hunting and fishing lands. But the native groups have no way of enforcing the ban.
The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species has banned the import to Europe of any and all products from grizzly hunts in British Columbia.
But the B.C. government intends to go ahead with its spring hunt.
